{"name":"Green Gate","hex":"#676957","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"gate","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A doorlike structure outside a house.","A doorway, opening, or passage in a fence or wall."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To keep something inside by means of a closed gate.","To punish (a student) by not allowing to leave the college grounds."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gate"}],"entries":[{"title":"Green Gate","blocks":[{"text":"The Green Gate in Gdańsk, Poland, is one of the city's most notable tourist attractions. It is situated between Long Market and the River Motława."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Gda%C5%84sk%2C_Brama_Zielona_%28HB1%29.jpg/330px-Gda%C5%84sk%2C_Brama_Zielona_%28HB1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Gate","wikidata":"Q1450669"},{"title":"Gate","blocks":[{"text":"A gate or gateway is a point of entry to or from a space enclosed by walls or other barriers. The word is derived from Proto-Germanic *gatan, meaning an opening or passageway. Synonyms include portal. The concept originally referred to the gap or hole in the wall or fence, rather than a barrier which closed it. Gates may prevent or control the entry or exit of individuals, or they may be merely decorative. The moving part or parts of a gateway may be considered \"doors\", as they are fixed at one side whilst opening and closing like one."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/Gate_of_the_BCR_Headquarters_Building_%28Bucharest%2C_Romania%29.jpg/330px-Gate_of_the_BCR_Headquarters_Building_%28Bucharest%2C_Romania%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gate","wikidata":"Q53060"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Relief_at_Green_Gate_in_Gda%C5%84sk_F.jpg/500px-Relief_at_Green_Gate_in_Gda%C5%84sk_F.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Relief_at_Green_Gate_in_Gda%C5%84sk_F.jpg/1280px-Relief_at_Green_Gate_in_Gda%C5%84sk_F.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Relief_at_Green_Gate_in_Gda%C5%84sk_F.jpg/500px-Relief_at_Green_Gate_in_Gda%C5%84sk_F.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"16th century coat of arms of Poland","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARelief_at_Green_Gate_in_Gda%C5%84sk_F.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Castel_B%C3%A9ranger%2C_February_16%2C_2013.jpg/500px-Castel_B%C3%A9ranger%2C_February_16%2C_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Castel_B%C3%A9ranger%2C_February_16%2C_2013.jpg/1280px-Castel_B%C3%A9ranger%2C_February_16%2C_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Castel_B%C3%A9ranger%2C_February_16%2C_2013.jpg/500px-Castel_B%C3%A9ranger%2C_February_16%2C_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Art Nouveau gate of Castel Béranger (Paris)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACastel_B%C3%A9ranger%2C_February_16%2C_2013.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Kebun_Raya_Bali_Candi_Bentar_IMG_8794.jpg/500px-Kebun_Raya_Bali_Candi_Bentar_IMG_8794.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Kebun_Raya_Bali_Candi_Bentar_IMG_8794.jpg/1280px-Kebun_Raya_Bali_Candi_Bentar_IMG_8794.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Kebun_Raya_Bali_Candi_Bentar_IMG_8794.jpg/500px-Kebun_Raya_Bali_Candi_Bentar_IMG_8794.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Candi bentar, a typical Indonesian gate that is often found on the islands of Java and Bali","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%